{2}^{n}}$" 13194139534533 true] [17592186293468 1113 #uuid "0860410e-f60a-5b80-a1c8-6a3d047379c9" 13194139534533 true] [17592186293468 1145 "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }0.25{(1.1)}^{n}$ starts with $n=0.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{{a}_{0}} & =\\frac{0.25{(1.1)}^{1}}{0.25{(1.1)}^{0}} \\\\  & =1.1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{4}{(-\\frac{5}{2})}^{n}$ starts with $n=1.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{4}{(-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}}{\\frac{1}{4}{(-\\frac{5}{2})}^{1}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }\\frac{{3}^{n-1}}{{2}^{n}}$ starts with $n=2.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(\\frac{{3}^{3-1}}{{2}^{3}})}{(\\frac{{3}^{2-1}}{{2}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{2}^{3}})}{(\\frac{3}{{2}^{2}})} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{3}^{2}}{{2}^{3}})(\\frac{{2}^{2}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"None.\"" 13194139534533 true] [17592186293469 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293470 13194139534533 true] [17592186293469 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293471 13194139534533 true] [17592186293469 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293472 13194139534533 true] [17592186293469 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293473 13194139534533 true] [17592186293469 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293474 13194139534533 true] [17592186293469 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534533 true] [17592186293469 1041 "selection" 13194139534533 true] [17592186293469 1104 #edb/ref 17592186293472 13194139534533 true] [17592186293469 1110 #uuid "a3f040b7-a861-517f-b2cc-87b018b09d90" 13194139534533 true] [17592186293470 1034 "I only" 13194139534533 true] [17592186