y }8{(2)}^{n}$\n- ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{8}^{n+1}}{{16}^{n-1}}$" 13194139534533 true] [17592186293527 1113 #uuid "f576e449-3f8d-544f-ad07-4e21e895f136" 13194139534533 true] [17592186293527 1145 "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}$ starts with $n=5.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{6}}{{a}_{5}} & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{2}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{6}}{\\frac{1}{2}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}^{5}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }8{(2)}^{n}$ starts with $n=1.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{8{(2)}^{2}}{8{(2)}^{1}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r|\\ge 1.$\n\n- The series ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{8}^{n+1}}{{16}^{n-1}}$ starts with $n=0.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{{a}_{0}} & =\\frac{\\frac{{8}^{1+1}}{{16}^{1-1}}}{\\frac{{8}^{0+1}}{{16}^{0-1}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{8}^{2}}{{16}^{0}})}{(\\frac{{8}^{1}}{{16}^{-1}})} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{8}^{2}}{{16}^{0}})(\\frac{{16}^{-1}}{{8}^{1}}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{{8}^{2}}{1})(\\frac{1}{16\\cdot 8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r|<1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only series  I and III converge." 13194139534533 true] [17592186293528 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293529 13194139534533 true] [17592186293528 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293530 13194139534533 true] [17592186293528 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293531 13194139534533 true] [17592186293528 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293532 13194139534533 true] [17592186293528 1000 #edb/ref 17592186293533 13194139534533 true] [17592186293528 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534533 true] [17592186293528 1041 "selection" 13194139534533 true] [17592186293528 1104 #edb/ref 17592186293530 13194139534533 true] [175